## Claude Code + shared knowledge: start here
# Every agent (Jarvis, Friday, Veronica) and every joined Claude Code seat
# reads and writes ONE knowledge tree. Joining a new box is one command.
# Not joining is how you get a clean slate - that's a feature, not a gap.

## Fastest path - new box, nothing installed
# Linux / macOS - detects the distro and uses apt / dnf / yum / brew for you
bash -c "$(curl -fsSL operations.dev/claude-join.sh)"

# Windows (PowerShell)
irm operations.dev/claude-win-join.ps1 | iex

# Both install what's missing, pause for the two logins that need a browser,
# then clone the tree, link it and write the session hooks.

## Prefer to see the steps yourself
curl operations.dev/claude-linux
curl operations.dev/claude-windows

## If this box also needs to DO homelab work
# Joining gives the session context, not credentials - nothing in the tree is
# a secret. When the work needs real homelab access (running a playbook,
# touching an API), add secrets separately:
curl operations.dev/doppler

## Already joined - the day-to-day
# Nothing. The hooks pull at session start and push at the end.
# Browse the facts in Obsidian: Jarvis/team-memory/internal (MEMORY.md is the index)

## Did it work / is this box already a seat
# Nothing about a running session announces that it is joined, so ask the disk,
# not the model. Lists every project claude knows about, which are joined, the
# seat name, and whether both hooks are actually wired:
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/seats.sh
pwsh ~/agent-notes/tooling/seats.ps1        # Windows

# Scriptable: exit 0 if any seat on this box is joined, 1 if none
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/seats.sh --quiet

## Which BOXES are enrolled across the fleet
# There is no registry - nothing reports in when a box joins. The only
# fleet-wide evidence is who has WRITTEN to the tree:
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/seats.sh --fleet

# Read it as contribution history, not a roster. A box retired months ago still
# appears; a box joined this morning that hasn't written yet does not. Use the
# first/last dates to judge. To settle one box, run seats.sh ON that box.

## The seat's name
# Signs this box's commits to the tree. Written by the join script, one line,
# untracked - so renaming is just editing the file:
cat  ~/agent-notes/.seat
echo desktop1-wsl > ~/agent-notes/.seat
# Unset means the box signs as "agent", same as every other unnamed seat.
# WSL TRAP: a WSL guest reports its WINDOWS HOST's name as its own hostname, so
# both default to the same seat name. The scripts suffix -wsl / -win; a box
# joined before that needs .seat set by hand on both sides.

## Leaving - retiring a seat from this box
# Joining leaves behind a private clone of the whole tree, a GitHub token and
# your Anthropic credential. rm -rf ~/.claude is the intuitive cleanup and it is
# the WRONG one: it deletes the memory POINTER and keeps the corpus, and leaves
# both credentials live. Use the script - dry run first, it changes nothing:
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/leave.sh
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/leave.sh --yes          # revoke and remove
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/leave.sh --yes --uninstall-cli

# Windows twin (built, not yet exercised end to end - check its output):
pwsh ~/agent-notes/tooling/leave.ps1

# It REFUSES while the tree holds facts that never reached GitHub. Push first:
bash ~/agent-notes/tooling/hooks/stop.sh
# The GitHub token stays valid server-side until revoked at
# github.com/settings/applications - that grant is shared by all your boxes.
# After retiring a box, delete or reword any fact describing that seat.

